This was my first Knight ascension, and my first attempt at wishless
conduct.  I like knights now; they start out a little strange (very
heavy armor, poor pet, and what's up with the lance?!) but they become
good characters later on (notably so after the quest).  I also like the
intrinsic jumping

I was priming my knight to be a spellcaster, so I swapped out all my
clunky metal armor for spell-friendly armor as soon as I could.  My
first spell was sleep, and it came in handy a few times.  Later,
though, I was just waiting for it to time out so I could use it as my
confusion spell.  It was a good thing it didn't, though, because it
saved me in the quest (I was a little over my head with Ixoth, until I
convinced him to take a nap and a beating).

I dipped for Excalibur as soon as I could, and got it pretty quickly.
That's probably the main advantage knights have in the early game,
since Excalibur is a decently powerful weapon.  I decided pretty early
on that I was going to try for wishless, then found a magic lamp
shortly thereafter in Minetown.  It took a lot of willpower not to use
it . . . but when I was later offered a throne wish and for the first
time answered "nothing", I knew it would be wishless or die trying.

Knights are very powerful once they have the Magic Mirror and the spell
of Magic Missile.  Those x2 missiles are powerful enough to kill almost
anything in a single shot, especially if they can be bounced off of a
wall.  I used Magic Missile a great deal, Identify a dozen or so times,
and Charm Monster a few times.

The hardest part about playing wishless was, to my surprise, not the
lack of equipment.  I eventually found almost everything I would have
wished for.  The hard thing in this game was the lack of enchantments .
. . I didn't see a single enchant armor scroll until well after the
quest, and even then I think I found or wrote a grand total of four in
the entire game.  I never found a charging scroll, so my one magic
marker was only useful for a short time.  AC -23, though, was
apparently good enough.

I started considering genoless when I still hadn't genocided anything
after the quest . . . my decision was helped along when my two scrolls
of genocide were wiped out due to a water accident.  I found another
one later, and just used it to create some nurses.  The non-polymorph
conducts, to me, aren't difficult . . . not changing form is mainly a
matter of luck in the early game, and not polymorphing objects doesn't
impact the game too much.

Overall, I think wishless conduct definitely adds a new dimension to
the game . . . I never truly felt the comfortable "walking tank"
feeling I've had on other ascensions, and the entire post-Rodney
ascension run had a feeling of mild panic to it.  I'll probably try
wishless again.



